{:edb/db-before-t 1128 :edb/db-after-t 1129 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534441 50 #inst "2026-10-07T04:10:27.178Z" 13194139534441 true] [17592186157040 1100 "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty)$, generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2837/Source/Images/b479d2e8e92d2b8a69192633d94f8399.png)" 13194139534441 false] [17592186157040 1100 "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/1a75a5242f3636efac8def7fc62458e1c1c6972ca79a1ab6fd73af47be622a7a.png)" 13194139534441 true] [17592186157040 1145 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-4,5)$, and passes through the point $(-2,3)$.\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = 2pt, \\qquad y - k = pt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (-4,5)\n$$\n\nand so we have\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2pt, \\qquad y - 5 = pt^2\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-2, 3)$, we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{2 = 2pt; - 2 = pt^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= 2pt \\\\\n1 &= pt \\\\\nt &= \\frac{1}{p}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2 &= p(\\frac{1}{p})^{2} \\\\\n- 2 &= \\frac{1}{p} \\\\\np &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t, \\qquad y - 5 = \\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t^2\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx + 4 = -t, \\qquad y - 5 = -\\dfrac12t^2\n$$" 13194139534441 false] [17592186157040 1145 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-4,5),$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = 2pt, \\qquad y - k = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-4,5),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2pt, \\qquad y - 5 = pt^2.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-2, 3),$ we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ -2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t, \\qquad y - 5 = \\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx + 4 = -t, \\qquad y - 5 = -\\dfrac12t^2 .\n$$" 13194139534441 true] [17592186157041 1100 "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$, generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2837/Source/Images/90c50a502dfbd7ddf0ed06b806d52370.png)" 13194139534441 false] [17592186157041 1100 "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/8355891fbc605593a32dd100ee8c1c2e4324da0630755984158ec23f6d419ab9.png)" 13194139534441 true] [17592186157041 1145 "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,2)$, and passes through the point $(1,0)$.\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = pt^2, \\qquad y - k = 2pt\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (-3,2)\n$$\n\nand so we have\n\n$$\nx+3 = pt^2, \\qquad y-2 = 2pt\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,0)$, we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{4 = pt^{2}; - 2 = 2pt\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2 &= 2pt \\\\\nt &= -\\frac{1}{p}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= p(-\\frac{1}{p})^{2} \\\\\n4 &= p \\cdot \\frac{1}{p^{2}} \\\\\n4 &= \\frac{1}{p} \\\\\np &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t^2, \\qquad y -2 = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\dfrac{1}{4}t^2, \\qquad y -2= \\dfrac{1}{2}t\n$$" 13194139534441 false] [17592186157041 1145 "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,0).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = pt^2, \\qquad y - k = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-3,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3 = pt^2, \\qquad y-2 = 2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,0),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(-\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t^2, \\qquad y -2 = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\dfrac{1}{4}t^2, \\qquad y -2= \\dfrac{1}{2}t .\n$$" 13194139534441 true] [17592186157042 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-1 = -2t^2, \\qquad y+1 = -4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$" 13194139534441 true] [17592186157042 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x-1 = -2t^2, \\qquad y+1 = -4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty)$." 13194139534441 false] [17592186157042 1145 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} &= -\\frac{x - 1}{2}, t =-\\frac{y + 1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} &= (-\\frac{y + 1}{4})^{2} \\\\\n&= ((y + 1)^{2})/(16)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation\n\n$$\nt^2 = -\\dfrac{x-1}{2}\n$$\n\nand rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((y + 1)^{2})/(16) &= -\\frac{x - 1}{2} \\\\\n(y + 1)^{2} &= 16 \\cdot (-\\frac{x - 1}{2}) \\\\\n(y + 1)^{2} &= -8(x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534441 false] [17592186157042 1145 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{y+1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = -\\dfrac{x-1}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2} \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{x-1}{2}) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =-8(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534441 true] [17592186157237 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288353 13194139534441 true] [17592186157237 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288360 13194139534441 true] [17592186157237 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288367 13194139534441 true] [17592186157237 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288425 13194139534441 true] [17592186157237 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288432 13194139534441 true] [17592186157238 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288374 13194139534441 true] [17592186157238 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288381 13194139534441 true] [17592186157238 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288388 13194139534441 true] [17592186157238 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288395 13194139534441 true] [17592186157238 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288439 13194139534441 true] [17592186157239 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288402 13194139534441 true] [17592186157239 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288409 13194139534441 true] [17592186157239 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288415 13194139534441 true] [17592186157239 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288420 13194139534441 true] [17592186157239 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288446 13194139534441 true] [17592186288353 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288354 13194139534441 true] [17592186288353 1089 "q-106447" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288353 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534441 true] [17592186288353 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/4ac7f5cd3405e3839b829edc19c440c973521a0263f1aaa0a2f8245697779561.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288353 1113 #uuid "fedc73e9-6bd7-5662-845d-cc5946c6553c" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288353 1145 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-1,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-1,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+1=2pt,\\,y-2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,4),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+1=2(\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y-2=(\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+1=t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288354 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288355 13194139534441 true] [17592186288354 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288356 13194139534441 true] [17592186288354 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288357 13194139534441 true] [17592186288354 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288358 13194139534441 true] [17592186288354 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288359 13194139534441 true] [17592186288354 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534441 true] [17592186288354 1041 "selection" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288354 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288355 13194139534441 true] [17592186288354 1110 #uuid "904337e3-8d48-5969-92a8-aaf05b0b62b4" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288355 1034 "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288355 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534441 true] [17592186288355 1111 #uuid "ad394608-abcb-5de2-b5ff-e06516eabe0b" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288356 1034 "x-1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288356 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534441 true] [17592186288356 1111 #uuid "1d2c872a-7d0c-506f-9378-3d42b3eee454" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288357 1034 "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2={t}^{2}" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288357 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534441 true] [17592186288357 1111 #uuid "835e0a1a-b723-5064-8e33-8987a3a18d7e" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288358 1034 "x+1=2t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288358 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534441 true] [17592186288358 1111 #uuid "6ecadcb3-f282-5d55-8bd9-2ec6296e661b" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288359 1034 "x+1=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288359 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534441 true] [17592186288359 1111 #uuid "8e36d544-da4c-5b38-a3f0-cea6d0790f3b" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288360 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288361 13194139534441 true] [17592186288360 1089 "q-106452" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288360 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534441 true] [17592186288360 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/0c37bb9e010bdb19de2afc8707b6cf1cef69638c9f4721c407c1593f3a387b87.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288360 1113 #uuid "3de3f5ae-3fc0-5c32-b996-56050a6bc315" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288360 1145 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-2,-3),$ and passes through the point $(-12,-13).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-2,-3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+2=2pt,\\,y+3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-12,-13),$ we can substitute $x=-12$ and $y=-13$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-10=2pt \\\\ -10=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =2pt \\\\ -5 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{5}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =p{(-\\frac{5}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -10 & =p\\cdot \\frac{25}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -10 & =\\frac{25}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+2=2(-\\frac{5}{2})t,\\,y+3=(-\\frac{5}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+2=-5t,\\,y+3=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288362 13194139534441 true] [17592186288361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288363 13194139534441 true] [17592186288361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288364 13194139534441 true] [17592186288361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288365 13194139534441 true] [17592186288361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288366 13194139534441 true] [17592186288361 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534441 true] [17592186288361 1041 "selection" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288361 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288363 13194139534441 true] [17592186288361 1110 #uuid "147672d7-0c0c-5a02-8cf9-6d414ff2f3b6" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288362 1034 "x+2=5t,\\,\\,y+3=\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288362 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534441 true] [17592186288362 1111 #uuid "6f9a4fa1-d8c3-588f-98fe-b81a5881a397" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288363 1034 "x+2=-5t,\\,\\,y+3=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288363 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534441 true] [17592186288363 1111 #uuid "258895d0-7b61-57ed-9571-b5c3ac4f8ea7" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288364 1034 "x+2=-\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,\\,y+3=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288364 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534441 true] [17592186288364 1111 #uuid "d0b852c5-550f-595e-98d5-79ff3935f2ea" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288365 1034 "x+2=5t,\\,\\,y+3=-2{t}^{2}" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288365 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534441 true] [17592186288365 1111 #uuid "73d5206d-fdbf-580d-ab23-fa43572f6fd8" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288366 1034 "x+2=-10t,\\,\\,y+3=-5{t}^{2}" 13194139534441 true] [17592186288366 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534441 true] [17592186288366 1111 #uuid "695f8cd3-739e-5183-b